                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite5/meta-rainiera6/recipes-rainier/plat-svc/files/rainier-early-sys-init",
                    "line": 142,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "But this isn't powering on... this is BMC init.  Why do we need to unbind?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite5/meta-rainiera6/recipes-rainier/plat-svc/files/rainier-early-sys-init",
                    "line": 142,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "RQLai",
                        "email": "r.q.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "11106313-RQLai"
                    },
                    "message": "Based on the CPLD config, the BMC can access the BIOS flash when the CPU is powered off. On the first boot after an AC cycle, the CPU remains off, allowing the BMC driver to initialize the flash.\nHowever, if the CPU is powered ON by the power button, the CPLD will switch the mux for the CPU to access the flash. This can cause a conflict if the BMC hasn't unbound the driver. To prevent resource conflicts, we have to unbind the driver before powering on the CPU."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite5/meta-rainiera6/recipes-rainier/plat-svc/files/rainier-early-sys-init",
                    "line": 142,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "I think this is a CPLD bug. Can we have it default access to the CPU? I dont think yosemite5 does this. So, I am not certain why Rainier is special."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite5/meta-rainiera6/recipes-rainier/plat-svc/files/rainier-early-sys-init",
                    "line": 142,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "RQLai",
                        "email": "r.q.lai@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "11106313-RQLai"
                    },
                    "message": "Apologies for the delayed response. We have already requested both the CPLD and hardware teams to work together on this modification.\nThis change is about whether the BIOS can boot into the OS successfully.\nAfter completing 25 BIOS update cycles, 1,000 DC cycles, and 150 AC cycles, the system can still successfully boot into the OS.\nWe will remove unSPI in the initial stage."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-yosemite5/meta-rainiera6/recipes-rainier/plat-svc/files/rainier-early-sys-init",
                    "line": 142,
                    "reviewer": {
